---
source: crates/safix/src/reporter.rs
expression: render_plain(&refusal)
---
safix: /home/alice/.keys/master.kdbx needs its password and there is no terminal to ask on. Nothing
was read.

The refusal is here rather than after the first mapping deliberately: a run
that prompted into the void would have decrypted safix's side of every
mapping first, and a run that treated the database as empty would report
every mapping as one-sided and, in backup mode, write.

There is one way to provide the database, and it is the declared path plus
the password on a terminal. The session's secret service is not a second
way: the collection it publishes is KeePassXC's exposed group, so it cannot
address the group and path a mapping declares.

Run this where you can type, or leave the mapping to a run that can.
